{"title":"Population & Property Dynamics","narrative":"The evacuation model instantiates 100 Person agents on a 40×40 grid with a single 3-cell door on the east wall. Each agent has a boolean property “has_exited,” and the environment tracks a numeric global property “evacuated_count.” The run proceeded for the full 300 configured steps without early termination, as indicated by run status “finished.”

Because the simulation did not stop early (which would occur if all agents evacuated), we infer that at least one Person remained inside at step 300. However, no time‐series data for population counts or evacuated_count were provided in the evidence, so the rate of evacuation, phases of rapid exit, or equilibrium levels cannot be quantified.

In the absence of detailed series, key dynamics such as throughput at the door (given 1.0 cells/step movement) and potential congestion effects remain speculative. To fully characterize growth/decline phases or tipping points, explicit time‐series of evacuated_count and in‐room population would be required.","key_findings":["100 Person agents were simulated; run reached 300 steps without early exit, implying some agents remained un‐evacuated","No aggregated time‐series for global "evacuated_count" or in‐room population was provided, preventing quantification of exit rates","Run status "finished" (not terminated) indicates the exit condition (all agents exited) was not met within 300 steps"],"causal_links":[],"emergent_phenomena":[]}
